(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Formulate Peter Mitchell's Chemiosmotic Hypothesis and explain the two physical components of the Proton Motive Force \(pmf\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Peter Mitchell's Chemiosmotic Hypothesis \(Nobel Prize 1978\):) Tj
T*
(   1. Foundational Postulates:) Tj
T*
(   - The inner mitochondrial membrane is fundamentally impermeable to hydrophilic ions,) Tj
T*
(   particularly protons \(H+\).) Tj
T*
(   - Electron transport through respiratory complexes \(I, III, and IV\) is coupled vectorially to) Tj
T*
(   the outward extrusion of protons from the mitochondrial matrix into the intermembrane space) Tj
T*
(   \(IMS\).) Tj
T*
(   - This asymmetrical proton extrusion establishes an electrochemical potential gradient of) Tj
T*
(   protons across the membrane, termed the Proton Motive Force \(pmf\).) Tj
T*
(   - The passive re-entry of protons down this electrochemical gradient through the) Tj
T*
(   membrane-embedded F0 sector of ATP synthase drives the mechanical synthesis of ATP in the) Tj
T*
(   catalytic F1 sector.) Tj
T*
(   2. Quantitative Components of the Proton Motive Force:) Tj
T*
(   The total electrochemical potential difference Delta mu_H+ is given by:) Tj
T*
(   Delta mu_H+ = F * Delta Psi - 2.303 * R * T * Delta pH.) Tj
T*
(   Dividing by the Faraday constant F expresses the proton motive force in electrical potential) Tj
T*
(   units \(volts or millivolts\):) Tj
T*
(   Delta p = Delta Psi - \(2.303 * R * T / F\) * Delta pH.) Tj
T*
(   - Membrane Electrical Potential \(Delta Psi\): Established by the separation of positive) Tj
T*
(   electrical charge across the low-dielectric lipid bilayer \(matrix inside negative, typically) Tj
T*
(   -150 to -180 mV\).) Tj
T*
(   - Chemical pH Gradient \(Delta pH = pH_matrix - pH_IMS\): Matrix is more alkaline than the IMS by) Tj
T*
(   approximately 0.75 to 1.0 pH units.) Tj
T*
(   - At 37 deg C \(310.15 K\), the factor \(2.303 * R * T / F\) equals 61.5 mV.) Tj
T*
(   - Total pmf: Delta p = \(-160 mV\) - \(61.5 mV * 0.75\) = -160 mV - 46.1 mV = -206.1 mV \(directed) Tj
T*
(   inwardly toward the matrix\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Describe the molecular architecture, redox centers, and electron transfer pathway of Complex I \(NADH:Ubiquinone Oxidoreductase\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Structure and Mechanism of Respiratory Complex I:) Tj
T*
(   1. Macromolecular Architecture:) Tj
T*
(   Complex I is an enormous L-shaped multimeric enzyme \(~1 MDa\) consisting of 45 distinct) Tj
T*
(   polypeptide subunits:) Tj
T*
(   - Hydrophilic Peripheral Arm: Extends ~14 nm into the mitochondrial matrix, containing the NADH) Tj
T*
(   binding site, FMN cofactor, and eight iron-sulfur \(Fe-S\) clusters.) Tj
T*
(   - Hydrophobic Membrane Arm: Spans the inner mitochondrial membrane \(~18 nm long\), composed of) Tj
T*
(   four proton-pumping modules homologous to bacterial Na+/H+ antiporters \(ND1, ND2, ND4, ND5\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Electron Transfer Pathway:) Tj
T*
(   - NADH binds near the top of the matrix arm, transferring two electrons and a proton as a) Tj
T*
(   hydride ion \(:H-\) to Flavin Mononucleotide \(FMN\), reducing it to FMNH2.) Tj
T*
(   - Electrons jump sequentially through a quantum mechanical tunneling wire of iron-sulfur) Tj
T*
(   clusters:) Tj
T*
(   FMNH2 -> N3 -> N1b -> N4 -> N5 -> N6a -> N6b -> N2.) Tj
T*
(   - Cluster N2 \(a [4Fe-4S] cluster\) is the terminal cluster with the highest reduction potential) Tj
T*
(   \(-140 mV\), positioned ~12 A from the ubiquinone binding pocket.) Tj
T*
ET

