(   [Problem Statement]: Detail Boyer's Binding Change Mechanism of F0F1 ATP Synthase and explain how gamma-shaft rotation drives ATP synthesis.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Paul Boyer's Binding Change Mechanism and Rotary Catalysis of ATP Synthase:) Tj
T*
(   1. Structural Organization:) Tj
T*
(   - F0 Sector \(Membrane Motor\): Composed of a ring of hydrophobic c-subunits \(c8 in mammals\) and) Tj
T*
(   a static a-subunit containing two non-connecting half-channels.) Tj
T*
(   - Central Rotor Stalk: The asymmetric gamma and epsilon subunits rigidly link the rotating) Tj
T*
(   c-ring to the catalytic core.) Tj
T*
(   - F1 Sector \(Catalytic Head\): A stationary hexamer of alternating alpha and beta subunits,) Tj
T*
(   \(alpha-beta\)3, held rigid against rotation by a peripheral stator stalk \(subunits b, d, F6,) Tj
T*
(   OSCP\). Only the three beta subunits contain catalytic active sites.) Tj
T*
(   2. The Three Conformational States of the Beta Subunit:) Tj
T*
(   Each beta subunit cycles through three distinct conformations dictated by the orientation of) Tj
T*
(   the asymmetric, bent gamma-shaft:) Tj
T*
(   - Open \(O\) State: Very low affinity for nucleotides; active site is wide open. Newly) Tj
T*
(   synthesized ATP dissociates into the matrix, and the empty site is ready for binding.) Tj
T*
(   - Loose \(L\) State: Binds ADP and inorganic phosphate \(Pi\) loosely and reversibly, holding them) Tj
T*
(   in close proximity without catalytic reaction.) Tj
T*
(   - Tight \(T\) State: Undergoes conformational compaction, binding ADP and Pi with picomolar) Tj
T*
(   affinity. The energy of binding forces substrate alignment, driving spontaneous, equilibrium) Tj
T*
(   synthesis of ATP without external energy input \(Delta G_catalytic approx 0\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Mechanical Rotation Drives Product Release:) Tj
T*
(   Proton flow through the F0 c-ring rotates the central gamma-shaft in 120-degree steps:) Tj
T*
(   - As the eccentric face of the gamma-shaft rotates into contact with a T-state beta subunit, it) Tj
T*
(   forces it open into the O-state, driving the energy-requiring step of ATP release!) Tj
T*
(   - Simultaneously: The O-state subunit transitions to L, and the L-state subunit transitions to) Tj
T*
(   T.) Tj
T*
(   - One complete 360-degree rotation of the gamma-shaft forces all three beta subunits through a) Tj
T*
(   full cycle, synthesizing and releasing exactly 3 molecules of ATP.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
