(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the biophysical operation of the two-half-channel model of the F0 sector and calculate the proton cost per ATP in mammals.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The Two-Half-Channel Model of F0 and Calculation of the H+/ATP Ratio:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Two-Half-Channel Mechanism \(Junge Model\):) Tj
T*
(   - Stator Subunit a contains two hydrophilic, non-coaxial aqueous half-channels that do not) Tj
T*
(   cross the membrane: an entry half-channel open to the IMS, and an exit half-channel open to the) Tj
T*
(   matrix.) Tj
T*
(   - In between, the rotating ring of c-subunits presents a conserved carboxylate residue \(Asp-61) Tj
T*
(   in bacteria, Glu-58 in mammals\) facing the membrane interior.) Tj
T*
(   - An unprotonated, negatively charged carboxylate \(-COO-\) cannot enter the hydrophobic lipid) Tj
T*
(   bilayer.) Tj
T*
(   - A proton from the IMS enters the entry half-channel, protonating the carboxylate to neutral) Tj
T*
(   \(-COOH\). In neutral form, the c-subunit can rotate out of subunit a and traverse the nonpolar) Tj
T*
(   lipid bilayer.) Tj
T*
(   - After completing nearly a full 360-degree rotation, the neutral c-subunit enters the exit) Tj
T*
(   half-channel.) Tj
T*
(   - Here, an invariant, positively charged Arginine-210 residue on subunit a electrostatically) Tj
T*
(   displaces the proton, forcing it into the matrix half-channel. The deprotonated c-subunit is) Tj
T*
(   now trapped until it rotates to the entry half-channel.) Tj
T*
(   2. Calculation of the H+/ATP Stoichiometric Ratio:) Tj
T*
(   - The mammalian c-ring possesses exactly 8 c-subunits \(c8 ring\).) Tj
T*
(   - One full 360-degree revolution requires the passage of 8 protons \(8 H+\), which drives the) Tj
T*
(   synthesis of 3 ATP molecules in the F1 sector:) Tj
T*
(   H+_c-ring / ATP = 8 / 3 = 2.67 H+ per ATP.) Tj
T*
(   - In addition, importing inorganic phosphate \(H2PO4-\) into the matrix by the phosphate) Tj
T*
(   translocase is an electroneutral symport driven by the co-import of 1 proton \(1 H+\):) Tj
T*
(   H+_phosphate / ATP = 1.0 H+.) Tj
T*
(   - Total Energetic Proton Cost per ATP:) Tj
T*
(   H+_total / ATP = 2.67 + 1.0 = 3.67 H+ per ATP.) Tj
T*
(   - Under standard NADH respiration \(10 H+ pumped / 2e-\): P/O ratio = 10 / 3.67 = 2.72) Tj
T*
(   \(experimentally ~2.5 ATP\).) Tj
T*
(   - Under FADH2 respiration \(6 H+ pumped / 2e-\): P/O ratio = 6 / 3.67 = 1.63 \(experimentally ~1.5) Tj
T*
(   ATP\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
