(   [Problem Statement]: Contrast the molecular mechanisms of respiratory uncouplers \(2,4-DNP, FCCP\) and ATP synthase inhibitors \(Oligomycin\) on mitochondrial respiration.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Mechanistic Differences Between Uncouplers and ATP Synthase Inhibitors:) Tj
T*
(   1. Chemical Uncouplers \(Protonophores, e.g., 2,4-Dinitrophenol - 2,4-DNP, FCCP\):) Tj
T*
(   - Mechanism: Lipophilic weak acids possessing an aromatic ring that delocalizes negative) Tj
T*
(   charge.) Tj
T*
(   - Action: In the acidic intermembrane space, the phenolate anion picks up a proton, becoming) Tj
T*
(   neutral \(DNP-H\). The neutral molecule diffuses freely across the inner mitochondrial membrane) Tj
T*
(   into the basic matrix, where it deprotonates, releasing the proton. The anionic phenolate) Tj
T*
(   diffuses back to the IMS, completing a catalytic cycle.) Tj
T*
(   - Effects on Bioenergetics:) Tj
T*
(   - Rapidly dissipates both Delta Psi and Delta pH \(collapses pmf to zero\).) Tj
T*
(   - Because pmf back-pressure is eliminated, electron transport and oxygen consumption \(State 4) Tj
T*
(   respiration\) accelerate to maximum velocity \(Vmax\).) Tj
T*
