(   [Problem Statement]: Compare C3, C4, and CAM photosynthetic adaptations regarding anatomy, initial carbon fixation, water use efficiency, and temperature response.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Comparative Analysis of C3, C4, and CAM Photosynthesis:) Tj
T*
(   1. C3 Pathway:) Tj
T*
(   - Initial Carboxylation: Rubisco fixes CO2 directly into 3-PGA in mesophyll cells.) Tj
T*
(   - Anatomy: Typical bifacial leaf anatomy lacking specialized bundle sheath chloroplasts.) Tj
T*
(   - Stomata Opening: Daytime.) Tj
T*
(   - Photorespiration: High \(up to 35% of carbon lost at > 30 deg C\).) Tj
T*
(   - Optimum Temperature: 15 to 25 deg C \(temperate crops: wheat, rice, barley, soybeans\).) Tj
T*
(   - Water Use Efficiency \(WUE\): Low \(400-500 g H2O lost per g CO2 fixed\).) Tj
T*
(   2. C4 Pathway:) Tj
T*
(   - Initial Carboxylation: Phosphoenolpyruvate \(PEP\) carboxylase fixes HCO3- into Oxaloacetate) Tj
T*
(   \(4C\) in mesophyll cells. PEP carboxylase has zero affinity for O2, eliminating oxygenase) Tj
T*
(   competition!) Tj
T*
(   - Anatomy: Kranz Anatomy \(wreath-like concentric rings of mesophyll surrounding thick-walled) Tj
T*
(   bundle sheath cells containing concentrated Rubisco\).) Tj
T*
(   - Mechanism: 4C organic acids \(malate or aspartate\) diffuse via plasmodesmata to bundle sheath) Tj
T*
(   cells, where malic enzyme decarboxylates them, concentrating CO2 around Rubisco \(up to 2,000) Tj
T*
(   ppm\), completely suppressing photorespiration.) Tj
T*
(   - Optimum Temperature: 30 to 45 deg C \(tropical grasses: maize, sugarcane, sorghum\).) Tj
T*
(   - Energetic Cost: Requires 2 extra ATP per CO2 fixed \(30 ATP/glucose vs 18 ATP for C3\), but) Tj
T*
(   highly advantageous under high light, heat, and drought.) Tj
T*
(   - WUE: Moderate-High \(250-350 g H2O per g CO2\).) Tj
T*
(   3. CAM Pathway \(Crassulacean Acid Metabolism\):) Tj
T*
(   - Temporal Separation: Stomata open exclusively at night to minimize transpiration.) Tj
T*
(   - Nocturnal Fixation: PEP carboxylase fixes CO2 into malate, stored in massive central vacuoles) Tj
T*
(   as malic acid \(leaf pH drops dramatically at night\).) Tj
T*
(   - Daytime Decarboxylation: Stomata close tightly. Vacuolar malate is exported and) Tj
T*
(   decarboxylated, flooding Rubisco with CO2 while light reactions supply ATP and NADPH.) Tj
T*
(   - Habitat: Arid succulents, desert cacti, pineapple, agaves.) Tj
T*
(   - WUE: Highest \(50-100 g H2O per g CO2 fixed\).) Tj
T*
