(   2. The Two Unique Bypass Enzymes:) Tj
T*
(   - Isocitrate Lyase: Cleaves isocitrate \(6C\) into Succinate \(4C\) and Glyoxylate \(2C\), bypassing) Tj
T*
(   the two oxidative decarboxylation steps.) Tj
T*
(   - Malate Synthase: Condenses Glyoxylate \(2C\) with a second molecule of Acetyl-CoA \(2C\) to form) Tj
T*
(   Malate \(4C\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Pathway Integration Across Compartments:) Tj
T*
(   - Glyoxysome \(Specialized Peroxisome\): Fatty acids undergo beta-oxidation to generate) Tj
T*
(   Acetyl-CoA. Acetyl-CoA condenses with oxaloacetate to form citrate, isomerized to isocitrate.) Tj
T*
(   Isocitrate lyase releases succinate. Malate synthase condenses glyoxylate with another) Tj
T*
(   Acetyl-CoA to make malate.) Tj
T*
(   - Mitochondria: Succinate exits the glyoxysome, enters the mitochondrial matrix, and is) Tj
T*
(   converted by TCA enzymes into Fumarate -> Malate -> Oxaloacetate.) Tj
T*
(   - Cytosol: Malate is exported to the cytosol, oxidized to oxaloacetate, and decarboxylated by) Tj
T*
(   Phosphoenolpyruvate Carboxykinase \(PEPCK\) to PEP, entering reverse glycolysis \(gluconeogenesis\)) Tj
T*
(   to yield sucrose.) Tj
T*
