(   [Problem Statement]: Discuss the molecular mechanism of Mitochondrial Permeability Transition Pore \(mPTP\) opening and its role in ischemic-reperfusion injury.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The Mitochondrial Permeability Transition Pore \(mPTP\) in Ischemia-Reperfusion:) Tj
T*
(   1. Molecular Composition and Characteristics:) Tj
T*
(   The mPTP is a high-conductance, non-selective channel spanning both mitochondrial membranes.) Tj
T*
(   While its exact subunit identity was historically debated, modern structural biology identifies) Tj
T*
(   it as a conformational rearrangement of the ATP synthase c-ring or dimers in association with) Tj
T*
(   Cyclophilin D \(CypD\) and the Adenine Nucleotide Translocase \(ANT\):) Tj
T*
(   - In closed state: High membrane potential Delta Psi and normal matrix pH maintain tight) Tj
T*
(   permeability barrier \(< 300 Da\).) Tj
T*
(   - In open state: Forms a massive aqueous pore with a molecular cutoff of ~1.5 kDa.) Tj
T*
(   2. Triggers for Pore Opening:) Tj
T*
(   - Pathological matrix calcium overload \([Ca2+]_matrix > 10 microM\).) Tj
T*
(   - Elevated reactive oxygen species \(ROS\) that oxidize critical cysteine residues on ANT and) Tj
T*
(   CypD.) Tj
T*
(   - Depletion of adenine nucleotides \(ATP, ADP\) and loss of matrix inorganic phosphate.) Tj
T*
(   3. Pathophysiology in Ischemia-Reperfusion Injury \(e.g., Myocardial Infarction\):) Tj
T*
(   - Ischemia Phase: Lack of oxygen halts oxidative phosphorylation. Glycolysis accumulates lactic) Tj
T*
(   acid; intracellular pH drops to < 6.5. Acidic pH strongly inhibits mPTP opening, keeping the) Tj
T*
(   pore closed despite high cytosolic Ca2+.) Tj
T*
(   - Reperfusion Phase: Sudden restoration of blood flow delivers oxygen and clears lactate,) Tj
T*
(   rapidly restoring physiological pH \(7.4\). Concurrently, the re-energized ETC generates massive) Tj
T*
(   bursts of ROS, and massive Ca2+ rushes into the mitochondrial matrix.) Tj
T*
(   - Catastrophic Opening: The combination of neutral pH, calcium overload, and ROS burst triggers) Tj
T*
(   sudden, irreversible opening of mPTP.) Tj
T*
(   - Consequences: Protons and solutes flood freely across the inner membrane, completely) Tj
T*
(   collapsing the membrane potential \(Delta Psi = 0\). Water rushes in, causing osmotic matrix) Tj
T*
(   swelling, cristae unfolding, and outer membrane rupture, dumping Cytochrome c and Smac/DIABLO) Tj
T*
(   into the cytosol, unleashing executioner caspase activation and widespread necrotic tissue) Tj
T*
(   death.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
