(   [Problem Statement]: Explain how the Malate-Aspartate Shuttle maintains cytosolic redox balance during rapid aerobic glycolysis.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Redox Homeostasis via the Malate-Aspartate Shuttle in Aerobic Glycolysis:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Glycolytic Requirement for Continuous NAD+:) Tj
T*
(   In the glycolytic pathway, Glyceraldehyde-3-Phosphate Dehydrogenase \(GAPDH\) requires a) Tj
T*
(   continuous supply of oxidized NAD+ to convert glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate into) Tj
T*
(   1,3-bisphosphoglycerate:) Tj
T*
(   GAP + NAD+ + Pi -> 1,3-BPG + NADH + H+.) Tj
T*
(   Because the total cytosolic pool of NAD+/NADH is small \(~10-20 microM\), unoxidized NADH) Tj
T*
(   accumulation rapidly halts glycolysis unless re-oxidized.) Tj
T*
(   2. Complete Cycle of the Malate-Aspartate Shuttle:) Tj
T*
(   - Step 1: Cytosolic Malate Dehydrogenase \(cMDH\) reduces Oxaloacetate \(OAA\) to Malate, using) Tj
T*
(   cytosolic NADH and regenerating NAD+ to sustain GAPDH activity.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 2: The Malate-alpha-Ketoglutarate Carrier \(SLC25A11\) translocates Malate across the) Tj
T*
(   inner membrane into the matrix in exchange for alpha-ketoglutarate.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 3: Mitochondrial Malate Dehydrogenase \(mMDH\) oxidizes Malate back to OAA, generating) Tj
T*
