(   [Problem Statement]: Describe the structural organization and function of the Peroxisomal Protein Import Machinery \(PEX genes and PTS1/PTS2 signals\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Peroxisome Biogenesis and Matrix Protein Import \(PTS1 and PTS2\):) Tj
T*
(   1. Peroxisomal Targeting Signals:) Tj
T*
(   Unlike mitochondria, peroxisomes import fully folded, and even oligomeric, co-factor-bound) Tj
T*
(   proteins post-translationally across their single membrane:) Tj
T*
(   - Peroxisomal Targeting Signal 1 \(PTS1\): A conserved, non-cleavable C-terminal tripeptide with) Tj
T*
(   the consensus sequence Ser-Lys-Leu \(SKL\) or conservative variants. Present on > 90% of) Tj
T*
(   peroxisomal matrix proteins \(catalase, urate oxidase\).) Tj
T*
(   - Peroxisomal Targeting Signal 2 \(PTS2\): A nonapeptide localized near the N-terminus with the) Tj
T*
(   consensus [R/K]-[L/V/I]-X5-[H/Q]-[L/A]. Present on thiolase.) Tj
T*
(   2. Cytosolic Receptors and Docking:) Tj
T*
(   - PEX5: The soluble cytosolic receptor for PTS1, binding the SKL tripeptide via its C-terminal) Tj
T*
(   tetratricopeptide repeat \(TPR\) domains.) Tj
T*
(   - PEX7: The cytosolic receptor for PTS2, requiring co-receptors \(PEX5L in mammals\).) Tj
T*
(   - Docking Complex: Cargo-bound PEX5 docks at the peroxisomal membrane with a docking complex) Tj
T*
(   consisting of PEX13, PEX14, and PEX17.) Tj
T*
(   3. Transient Pore Formation and Cargo Translocation:) Tj
T*
(   - Upon docking, PEX5 inserts into the membrane alongside PEX14, forming a dynamic, transient,) Tj
T*
(   gated translocation pore that expands to accommodate large folded proteins.) Tj
T*
(   - The cargo is discharged into the peroxisomal lumen.) Tj
T*
